  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 522 AM MDT Thu Jul 23 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nSlightly drier air moves in today behind the exiting shortwave; \nhowever, another shortwave moves in from the southwest and brings \nmore widely scattered showers and storms to much of the forecast \narea. Forecast soundings continue to show inverted V profiles, \nwhich will result in stronger thunderstorm outflow winds than the \nlast couple of days. ML CAPE values generally running between 800 \nand 1200 J/kg will also be sufficient for hail, brief heavy \ndownpours, and cloud to ground lightning. The combination of the \nthinning moisture and the building ridge will also warm afternoon \ntemperatures well into the 90s and even 100s for most lower \nelevation locations. \n\nA drier southwesterly flow aloft will continue to thin the \nmoisture profile from west to east on Friday and Saturday for \nmostly dry conditions and intensifying heat, but there will be \nenough lingering instability and moisture for at least some \nshowers and storms. Model guidance has steadily increased \nSaturday's thunderstorm activity over central and southwest MT \nwith a more defined shortwave passage during peak diurnal heating.\nOtherwise, strengthening dry southwesterly flow will increase \nsurface winds for at least the first half of the weekend and \nintroduce elevated to critical fire weather concerns, especially \nfor northern areas. \n\nSouthwesterly flow aloft continues over the Northern Rockies \nheading into next week. This will maintain the summer heat while \npassing shortwaves interact with diurnal instability for at least \ndaily isolated shower and thunderstorm activity. There will also \nbe periodic breeziness to go along with the hot and mostly dry \nconditions, but there's no clear signal for wind speeds strong \nenough to warrant fire weather concerns just quite yet. - RCG \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nShower and thunderstorm activity will be far more scattered in \nnature than yesterday, but the risk area will cover virtually the \nentire forecast area, not just central and southwest MT. Thinning \nmoisture profiles will begin to reduce the threat for localized \nflooding while increasing instances of strong wind gusts near \nshowers and storms. In addition to the strong wind gust threat \nstorms will also be capable of hail, brief downpours, and cloud to\nground lightning. Peak timing will generally run between 3 pm and\nmidnight.\n\nEven drier air moves in on Friday while the heat intensifies. \nIsolated showers and storms will mostly be confined to the central\nand southwest mountains. A more defined shortwave looks to bring \na slight uptick in shower and thunderstorm activity on Saturday. \nCentral and southwestern areas look to see the most widespread \nactivity with storm hazards including localized strong wind gusts,\nhail, and downpours in addition to lightning. - RCG",
